RESEARCH REFLECTION for lr_pair_scorer_loop2b_001:
Conclusion  : Good. lr_auprc=0.560 is far above the hypothesis target (~0.2 or higher) and the 0.11 random level, about 5x random. No baseline or prior best exists in the context, so is_new_best=True only means it is the first scored run. AUROC is 0.866 and direction_accuracy is 0.80, so the ranking signal is strong. The health gates passed, with 10 distinct score symbols and a dominant tie fraction of 0.10.
Key Factor  : Skip-anchored covariate-adjusted Spearman features fed to a small MLP (1538 params, smooth_l1 loss, cosine-decayed lr from 1e-3, 30 epochs) trained at formal_portion=1.0.
Discovery   : A tiny 1.5k-parameter MLP on covariate-adjusted Spearman features generalizes well. Train loss fell from 0.0476 to 0.0209 and validation from 0.0245 to 0.0217. The final train-validation gap is small (+0.0008, comparable). Validation was best at epoch 18, and the +3.6e-5 drift after that is negligible, so there is no meaningful overfitting. Validation was noisy in the high-lr middle epochs (a spike to 0.0239 at epoch 7) and settled as the lr decayed. Most of the loss gain came in the first 2 epochs, and the last ~10 epochs added almost nothing. The score exceeded the hypothesis by a wide margin. No Spearman-only baseline score was recorded, so the MLP's gain over plain Spearman is not established.
Memory Update: Use this run as the reference config: smooth_l1, about 1.5k params, 30 epochs, lr 1e-3 with cosine decay, lr_auprc 0.560, AUROC 0.866, direction_accuracy 0.80, final smooth_l1 loss 0.0209. Next steps: (1) Run the plain Spearman baseline, without the MLP and with the same eval, to measure the MLP's actual lift. Without it the efficiency and superiority claims are unsupported. (2) Test fewer epochs (about 15-20), since validation plateaued by epoch 18. This could give a more efficient config at the same score. (3) Only after that, try modest capacity or feature changes, such as the skip-anchoring variant or a wider hidden layer, one at a time. Judge them by lr_auprc, not smooth_l1 loss. Formal scope is operator-fixed at portion 1.0, so do not recommend changing trial_portion. Single-seed results can't resolve small differences, so repeat with a different seed before trusting gains of a few percent.
