RESEARCH REFLECTION for lr_pair_scorer_loop2b_002:
Conclusion  : Good, but only a marginal gain. lr_auprc 0.5643 is a new best, above the previous best of 0.5604 (+0.7%) and inside the hypothesised 0.56-0.58 range. No baseline score was supplied. This is a formal round at full portion, so data volume is not a concern. The gain is small enough that it may be seed noise. Secondary metrics are healthy: lr_auroc 0.868 and direction_accuracy 0.788. Health gates passed.
Key Factor  : The change was a slightly wider model with a higher peak lr and best-validation checkpointing. The settings were lr 0.002 (previously 0.001), 25 epochs with a cosine schedule, and a model of 5122 params. Of these, the higher lr with a shorter schedule is the most likely contributor. The checkpoint policy chose epoch 21 (reported as best ep 20 in the dynamics line, probably a 0-based vs 1-based indexing difference).
Discovery   : Training and validation both fell steadily. Train went 0.0341 to 0.0206 and validation went 0.0234 to 0.0216, so the run was not overfitting. After the best epoch, validation drifted up by only about 4e-5, so best-validation checkpointing barely changed which weights were kept. The train-validation gap was small at +0.00095. Validation was noisy in the high-lr phase, with spikes at epochs 6 and 3 (0.0236, 0.0226), and it settled once the cosine lr decayed. The final smooth_l1 train loss of 0.02062 is the lowest seen under this loss type, against 0.02090 for the previous best. The model is small (5122 params) and 25 epochs is fewer than the previous 30. The efficiency flag is True, but no baseline params were supplied, so the ratios are unavailable. Treat it as an efficient candidate for solidification, not a confirmed High-Efficiency Discovery. The loss improvement is tiny, and the score gain is only 0.0039, so the two cannot yet be tied causally.
Memory Update: Treat lr 0.002, cosine, 25 epochs, with a hidden_dim of about 32-48 and 2 layers, as the current best recipe (0.5643). It is a candidate for solidification. Next steps: (1) Confirm it with a repeat run on a different seed, since the 0.7% gain is within plausible seed noise. (2) If it holds, explore capacity (larger hidden_dim or num_layers) and a modest lr_schedule or epochs change. Validation is still improving slowly at the end of the schedule, so a longer horizon (35-40 epochs) at lr 0.002 is a reasonable test. (3) Do not change loss_type, because smooth_l1 is working and its losses are comparable across runs. (4) Do not suggest trial_portion changes, because the formal portion is operator-set at 1.0. (5) Change one factor at a time, so that gains can be attributed.
